Next, we want to model the effect of altitude, while accounting for time using INLA.

Seasonal trends

Distribution of overall sampling effort


Code
site_sampling_effort &lt;- data |&gt;
  group_by(site_id) |&gt;
  summarise(sampling_effort = n()) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(sampling_effort))

ggplot(site_sampling_effort, aes(x = sampling_effort)) +
  geom_histogram()











Station most sampled
We see clear seasonal pattern with a clear peak of nitrites and nitrates after summer that decreases in autumn. We also notice that ammonium seems to peak just before, or long after nitrites and nitrates. This could be explained by the lifecycle of nitrogen compounds (check references).


Code
site_most_point &lt;- site_sampling_effort |&gt;
  slice(1) |&gt;
  pull(site_id) |&gt;
  first()

data_most_point &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(site_id == site_most_point) |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) &gt; 2015)

ggplot(data_most_point, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line()











Station sampled randomly
We sample random stations and plot their 2020 seasonal trend, to get an idea of the sampling effort during the year, per variable.


Code
data_2020 &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) == 2020)

selected_sites &lt;- data_2020 |&gt;
  pull(site_id) |&gt;
  unique() |&gt;
  sample(9)

selected_data &lt;- data_2020 |&gt;
  filter(site_id %in% selected_sites)

ggplot(selected_data, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~site_id, scales = "free")









Code
ggplot(
  selected_data |&gt; filter(parameter_code %in% c("NH4", "PO4")),
  aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~site_id, scales = "free")









We can also have an idea of the average sampling effort per month per variable, to assess whether some nutrients are sampled during specific period each year, or not.


Code
sampling_per_month &lt;- data |&gt;
  mutate(month = as.factor(lubridate::month(date, label = TRUE))) |&gt;
  left_join(
    data |&gt;
      group_by(parameter_code) |&gt;
      summarise(n_tot = n(), .groups = "drop"),
    by = join_by(parameter_code)
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(parameter_code, month, n_tot) |&gt;
  summarise(sampling_effort = n(), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(
    rel_sampling_effort = sampling_effort / n_tot
  )

ggplot(
  sampling_per_month,
  aes(x = month, y = rel_sampling_effort, color = parameter_code, group = parameter_code)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  labs(color = "Parameter", x = "Month", y = "Average sampling effort")









The average sampling effort appears to be relatively even for nutrients.

Sampling methods

Overview over all stations
Raw yearly counts per method are dominated by the sampling effort trend above, which makes method changes hard to spot. We instead look at the share of observations per method each year, for each parameter.


Code
method_share &lt;- riversea::summarise_rephy_method_share(raw_data) |&gt;
  mutate(method = stringr::str_replace(
    method,
    "Spectrophotométrie",
    "Sp."
  )) |&gt;
  filter(year &gt;= 2000)

riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(
  method_share |&gt;
    filter(parameter_code == "NH4")
)









