We notice a change in measurement methods for all parameters around 2006.


Focus on one station
We can try to see if this change of method is reflected in nutrient concentration by looking at a station where the change occurred.


Code
site &lt;- site_most_point

data_site &lt;- raw_data |&gt;
  filter(`Lieu de surveillance : Identifiant` == site)

method_share_site &lt;- riversea::summarise_rephy_method_share(data_site) |&gt;
  mutate(method = stringr::str_replace(
    method,
    "Spectrophotométrie",
    "Sp."
  )) |&gt;
  filter(year &gt;= 2000)

riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(method_share_site)









So for this station there is a clear change in sampling method. Let’s see if it is reflected in nutrient values.


Code
data_most_point &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(site_id == site_most_point) |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) &gt;= 2003 & lubridate::year(date) &lt;= 2012)

ggplot(data_most_point, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line()









Luckily, we see that the change of methods doesn’t seem to cause a change in nutrient values.

Demersal/pelagic position by survey
Same idea, but for demers_pelag and grouped directly by survey rather than by the freshwater/marine/migratory origin above: for each survey, the share of distinct species falling into each demersal/pelagic category.


Code
species_survey &lt;- size |&gt;
  distinct(species_valid, survey)

survey_demers_pelag &lt;- species_survey |&gt;
  left_join(
    species_traits |&gt; select(species, demers_pelag),
    by = c("species_valid" = "species")
  ) |&gt;
  count(survey, demers_pelag) |&gt;
  group_by(survey) |&gt;
  mutate(share = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

ggplot(survey_demers_pelag, aes(x = survey, y = share, fill = demers_pelag)) +
  geom_col(width = 0.6) +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Set3") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Share of species", fill = "Position")









nurse and pomet look alike: both dominated by demersal species (~60%), with the rest split across pelagic-neritic and reef-associated. river is the opposite - mostly benthopelagic (61%) and demersal (31%), with almost no pelagic-neritic and, unsurprisingly, no reef-associated or bathydemersal species at all.
